7.1.30 G66 Modal Macro Subprogram Call Option / G67 Modal 
Macro Subprogram Call Cancel(Group 00)
G66 works similarly to G65 but is modal. It turns on calls for a subprogram with the ability
to pass arguments to it. It is canceled with G67. The format follows:
G66 Pnnnnn [Lnnnn] [arguments];
Arguments italicized in square brackets are optional. See the Programming section for
more details on macro arguments.
The G66 command requires a P address corresponding to a program number currently
located in the control’s drive or path to a program. When the L address is used, the macro
call is repeated the specified number of times.
Like G65 calls, G66 can be nested up to nine times, which means program 1 can call
program 2, program 2 can call program 3, and program 3 can call program 4.
When a subprogram is called, the control looks for it on the active drive or the path to the
program. If it cannot be located on the active drive, the control looks in the drive designated
by Setting 251. Refer to the Setting Up Search Locations section for more information on
subprogram searching. An alarm occurs if the control does not find the subprogram.
If the subprogram program specified by the P code is not should reside on the active drive
or on a drive designated by Setting 252.
In the example, subprogram 9010 is called once at each XY location with conditions passed
to the subprogram, “a b d f r”.
Example 1:
%
G66 P9010 a b d f r;M30 (Program stop) ;
XY (Call O09010);
XY (Call O09010);

